Description: Patrone's desktops: one per account, on the screen and remotely
 Each account has one desktop, which keeps running whether or not anyone is
 looking at it. The machine's own screen (the "Patrone Desktop" login
 session) and remote desktop clients are both views of it, and whoever
 connects last has it: a remote sign-in takes the screen back to the login
 screen, and signing in on the screen disconnects the remote client. It
 works the way users of Windows' Remote Desktop expect.
 .
 RDP clients, such as Windows' Remote Desktop Connection, sign in with the
 account's own username and password, which PAM checks on every connection.
 Only accounts in the remote-desktop group may, and only after an
 administrator turns it on: with the Remote Desktop switch in Settings'
 System > Remote Desktop (Patrone's build of GNOME Settings), or
 "patrone-remote-desktop enable".
 .
 On Hyper-V, VMConnect's enhanced session (Remote Desktop over a Hyper-V
 socket, which only the Hyper-V host can open) shows a login screen in its
 window. Any account that may sign in at the computer signs in there with
 its own password, checked by PAM, and gets its own desktop, with the
 clipboard, sound and a screen that follows the window, whether or not
 Remote Desktop is turned on.
 .
 Windows and Hyper-V are trademarks of the Microsoft group of companies.
